// U5 L3 Chronic Disease, Heredity, and Preventive Health — 30-question bank


::U5L3 Q01::Which statement best defines a chronic disease? {=A condition that often lasts a long time and may require ongoing management ~An illness that always spreads person to person ~A temporary injury that heals in one day ~A diagnosis based only on family history}

::U5L3 Q02::Which term is most similar to chronic disease in this lesson? {=Noncommunicable disease ~Pathogen ~Contagion ~Outbreak}

::U5L3 Q03::A risk factor is best described as {=a condition or behavior associated with a higher chance of an outcome ~proof that a person has a disease ~a guaranteed cause of disease ~a medical diagnosis}

::U5L3 Q04::Which is a protective factor? {=Regular physical activity ~Tobacco exposure ~Untreated high blood pressure ~Skipping recommended screenings}

::U5L3 Q05::Which is generally a modifiable risk factor? {=Tobacco use ~Age ~Inherited gene variant ~Family history}

::U5L3 Q06::Which is generally a nonmodifiable risk factor? {=Family history ~Sleep routine ~Physical activity ~Food choices}

::U5L3 Q07::Heredity refers to {=traits passed biologically from parents to children ~a person's daily choices only ~all environmental exposures ~a medical treatment plan}

::U5L3 Q08::Family history can help a person {=discuss possible risk and screening needs with a healthcare provider ~prove that disease is unavoidable ~diagnose themself ~ignore modifiable factors}

::U5L3 Q09::Which conclusion is supported when a fictional person has one risk factor? {=The factor may affect probability, but it does not prove disease ~The person definitely has the disease ~The person caused the disease ~Screening is never useful}

::U5L3 Q10::Primary prevention aims to {=reduce risk before disease develops ~confirm a diagnosis after symptoms ~replace professional care ~guarantee that disease never occurs}

::U5L3 Q11::Secondary prevention most directly includes {=screening and early detection ~choosing inherited traits ~avoiding all healthcare visits ~diagnosing friends}

::U5L3 Q12::A screening result is {=information that may show a need for further evaluation ~always a final diagnosis ~proof of personal blame ~a treatment by itself}

::U5L3 Q13::Which professional is qualified to interpret screening results and diagnose disease? {=An appropriate healthcare provider ~A classmate ~A social-media influencer ~Any family member}

::U5L3 Q14::Which is an environmental factor? {=Long-term exposure to polluted air ~Biological age ~An inherited gene ~Family history alone}

::U5L3 Q15::Which pairing is accurate? {=Blood pressure—cardiovascular health indicator ~Cholesterol—infectious pathogen ~Cancer—communicable disease ~Diabetes—temporary injury}

::U5L3 Q16::Cardiovascular disease affects primarily the {=heart and blood vessels ~skin only ~bones only ~digestive tract only}

::U5L3 Q17::Diabetes involves difficulty regulating {=blood glucose ~bone length ~eye color ~hearing range}

::U5L3 Q18::A respiratory disease primarily affects {=breathing and the lungs or airways ~hair growth ~bone density only ~tooth color}

::U5L3 Q19::A neurological disease primarily affects the {=brain, spinal cord, or nerves ~lungs only ~skin only ~teeth only}

::U5L3 Q20::Which response avoids blame? {=Risk develops through interacting biological, environmental, and behavioral factors ~Anyone with disease made bad choices ~Family history guarantees disease ~One behavior explains every case}

::U5L3 Q21::Why should multiple factors be considered together? {=Chronic-disease risk usually reflects interacting influences ~One factor always proves a diagnosis ~Environment never matters ~Protective factors cannot affect risk}

::U5L3 Q22::Which is an example of early detection? {=Completing a recommended blood-pressure screening ~Waiting until severe symptoms occur ~Diagnosing oneself online ~Ignoring an abnormal result}

::U5L3 Q23::Which action is an appropriate response to an abnormal screening result? {=Follow up with a qualified healthcare provider ~Assume a final diagnosis immediately ~Hide the result ~Give medical advice to classmates}

::U5L3 Q24::Access barriers may include {=cost, transportation, availability, or language support ~eye color and height ~only personal motivation ~a guaranteed diagnosis}

::U5L3 Q25::Which statement about genetics is accurate? {=Genes can influence risk without determining every outcome ~Genes are always modifiable ~Genes make prevention useless ~Genes are the same as a diagnosis}

::U5L3 Q26::Which plan combines primary and secondary prevention? {=Regular activity plus recommended screening ~Self-diagnosis plus no follow-up ~Ignoring family history and symptoms ~Waiting for an emergency only}

::U5L3 Q27::What is the strongest evidence-based conclusion from a family history of cardiovascular disease? {=The person may benefit from discussing risk and screening with a provider ~The person definitely has cardiovascular disease ~No lifestyle factor matters ~Treatment should begin without evaluation}

::U5L3 Q28::Which term means identifying a condition through professional evaluation? {=Diagnosis ~Screening ~Risk factor ~Protective factor}

::U5L3 Q29::Treatment is best defined as {=care intended to manage or improve a diagnosed health condition ~a guarantee of prevention ~a family-history record ~a self-screening conclusion}

::U5L3 Q30::Which analysis shows the strongest critical thinking? {=It names the evidence, classifies each factor, explains limits, and recommends appropriate prevention or follow-up ~It makes a diagnosis from one clue ~It blames the fictional person ~It lists terms without connecting them to evidence}
